{"description":"M1 (sto:knowledge-path-binding) granularity spike. Before the Standard 1.5 applicability field ships in wider use, measure on THIS repository how many constraints a typical changed file pulls in through `eka context --for`, so the granularity decision (how wide a declared scope should be: too wide is a gate an agent learns to ignore, too narrow never activates) has evidence rather than taste.","investigationNotes":"Method (dogfood, not simulation). (1) Declare `appliesTo` on a representative 21-artifact subset of this repository's own constraint corpus, spanning strata and widths: std (2: development-rules -\u003e cmd/**,bootstrap/**,feedback/**,scripts/**; eka-standard-1.0.0 -\u003e exchange/**), spec (3), adr (11 core ADRs mapped to the code they govern: 019 transitions/notes, 021 context, 022 skill pack -\u003e templates/**, 026 feedback, 027 snapshot transport -\u003e exchange/snapshots/** + sync/snapshot/import/export, 029 assigned, 031 plugin, 032 sync adoption, 035 capture, 036 status, 038 telemetry), dec (2: code discovery, m3a verify -\u003e cmd/verify.go), req (3: cli-experience -\u003e cmd/**, agent-self-describing-surface -\u003e AGENTS.md, version-surface-drift -\u003e version/update). All 20+1 revisions published as new instances (v2) carrying their state, relationships and change-log forward, then `eka sync adopt`ed onto this repository. (2) Run the real `eka context --for \u003cpath\u003e --json` over tracked files and count constraints. EXAMPLE: `eka context --for cmd/verify.go` -\u003e {dec:m3a-verify-staging}. `eka verify` scope-declarations PASS (every declared pattern matches at least one tracked path). RESULTS — all 93 code files (cmd/, bootstrap/, feedback/, scripts/; .go, non-test): min=1, median=2, max=4, mean=2.23; distribution: 9 files pull 1, 79 pull 2-3, 5 pull 4-5; none pull 0 or 6+. A 368-file stratified sample of the whole tree: min=0, median=2, max=4, mean=2.00. Snapshot-corpus files (exchange/snapshots/**) each pull exactly 2 (adr:027 + std:eka-standard-1.0.0); files outside every declared scope (docs/, templates/ parents, misc) pull 0. The heavy individual binders are the two broad `exchange/**` declarations (264 and 260 sampled files) and the two `cmd/**` declarations (94, 90).","conclusion":"A typical code file binds 2 constraints (median), never fewer than 1, at most 4 — the granularity is LOW-NOISE, not a gate an agent would learn to ignore. The evidence supports narrow, subsystem-scoped declarations (one ADR -\u003e the handful of files it governs): the corpus stays useful and each file still surfaces its governing constraint. The two broad `exchange/**` scopes are the only source of any concentration (2/file over the snapshot corpus) and are acceptable because the snapshot corpus IS the governed artifact. Decision: proceed with appliesTo at subsystem granularity; do not default to `**` (a repo-wide wildcard would pull every constraint on every file and is the failure mode this spike rules out). Caveat: this subset declares 21 of ~117 constraint artifacts; a full-corpus declaration would raise the per-file count, but the width discipline (narrow scopes) bounds it, which is exactly what the numbers show."}